  cites Diag chapter DiagStage

 Track B's B3 as a stage: one real TCP conversation with a real peer over
 the Intel part. Every stage before it reads the part; this one is the first
 that asks whether the STACK works on it, and it is the last stage in the
 ladder for that reason.

 It runs the production path and not a private one. `net-driver-bring-up`
 finds the card, calls `e1000-init` and calibrates the poll clock, exactly as
 `cdx-announce` and every server does, so a green row here is a statement
 about the code B4 will ship rather than about a bespoke arrangement. That
 costs a second CTRL.RST after nicinit's, which nicring refused for good
 reason at sitting 4 -- but nicring's question was what the PART does and
 this one's is what the STACK does, and the stack cannot be asked without
 the bring-up it actually uses. Every earlier stage has banked by the time
 this runs (L-BANK), so the reset is spent after the readings are safe.

 WHAT IT DOES NOT PROVE. The conversation is raw TCP: bytes out, bytes back.
 It does not speak the repository wire, so it is not B4 step 6, and an `ok`
 here says the peer was reached and answered and nothing about what it said.
 The `expect=` knob is the only content test and it is off by default.

 Every wait is budgeted against the HPET or against NetIO's tick count, and
 the stage refuses rather than spins when there is no clock: the ten-minute
 grey row on the I219-V is what that rule is made of.

 We say:

Section: States

  db3-states : List Text = ["ok", "short", "mismatch", "no-reply", "refused", "no-arp", "no-lease", "no-link", "no-card", "no-peer", "no-address", "no-part", "rejected", "bar-bad", "no-hpet", "clock-stuck"]

 `short` IS THE SEND SIDE SAYING SO, and until 2026-08-21 the stage could not
 say it. The exchange sent through `net-io-send-raw`, which hands back a bare
 transport, so `sent=` printed the length of the payload we INTENDED to send
 and would have printed it unchanged had the drain given up with the
 retransmit queue full and half the bytes still in hand. `expect=` cannot
 cover for that: it tests the REPLY, and it is off by default, so the row
 would have read `ok` on a truncated conversation. That is L-REFUSED in the
 one stage the flight is for. `net-io-send-raw-checked` answers how many
 bytes actually went, and `sent=` is now that number.

 The order the run tries them in is the order a reader eliminates classes:
 nothing was asked, no address to ask FROM, no part on the bus, a part we will
 not touch, a part whose window is wrong, no clock to budget with, the driver
 would not bind, no link, no lease when one was asked for, nobody answered ARP
 for the next hop, the handshake never completed, the peer said nothing, the
 peer said the wrong thing, ok.

 `ip=` IS REQUIRED AND THERE IS NO DEFAULT, which is a change of 2026-08-20.
 It used to fall back to 10.0.2.15, and that is not a default: it is the QEMU
 NAT guest address, so it is A GUESS ABOUT THE NETWORK that happens to be
 right in the bed and is off-subnet on any real switch.

 WHAT THAT COST IS A WRONG VERDICT, not a wrong-looking row. On metal without
 `ip=` the ARP still succeeds -- the request's target is the hop, so the hop
 answers whatever our sender address claims -- and then the SYN goes out from
 an address that belongs to nobody, no SYN-ACK comes back, and the stage
 answers `refused`. `refused`'s verdict row then tells the reader to check the
 peer address, the port, and reachability from that segment, ALL THREE OF
 WHICH ARE CORRECT. It does not merely mislead; it dispatches a person
 standing at the box to search the one place the fault is not.

 SO THIS REPAIRS `refused` RATHER THAN ADDING A STATE. Removing the one cause
 of `refused` that is ours means a future stick saying `refused` is saying
 something about the peer.

 A malformed value is the same answer. `ip=` that does not parse to four
 octets is `no-address` too, because a half-parsed address is the same class
 of lie as an invented one.

 `ip=dhcp` IS THE ONE WAY TO NOT TYPE AN ADDRESS, and it is a request rather
 than a fallback. The difference is who chose it: a composer who writes
 `ip=dhcp` has entered DHCP's failure modes deliberately, where a fallback
 enters them for somebody who typed nothing and expects nothing. So absent
 `ip=` the answer is still `no-address`, and it always will be.

 `no-lease` IS NOT `no-address`. An address was asked for FROM THE SEGMENT and
 no server answered, which is a fact about the segment; `no-address` is a fact
 about DIAG.CFG. A reader who cannot tell those apart is sent to the wrong end
 of the wire, which is the whole complaint that deleted the old default.

Section: Knobs

 One ARP request per round and one clock read per round, so the chunk size
 IS the request rate.

 THE POLL COST IS TWO NUMBERS AND THIS SECTION USED TO QUOTE ONE OF THEM
 UNLABELLED, which is how it got read back as the wrong one. `nicsit` measures
 an empty e1000 poll at **13.0 ns in the bed** and **32.9 ns on metal**: its own
 row bakes the reference `(bed 13034us / 1303us)` for a million polls, and the
 board answered 32935us on 2026-08-21, which is the 2.53x red recorded. Metal is
 the one a flight pays, so both are given here and each says which it is.

 Eight million polls is therefore about **104 ms in the bed and 263 ms on
 metal**. Inside the two-second budget that is at most about nineteen requests
 on the wire in the bed and about EIGHT on the board, not the twenty this said
 when it quoted the bed figure as if it were general. The conclusion is
 unchanged and the margin is wider: a 512-poll chunk copied from the ring stage
 would still have put four thousand broadcasts on a live segment in a fraction
 of a second, which is a flood and not a probe.

  db3-chunk : Integer = 8000000
  db3-arp-rounds : Integer = 32
  db3-src-port : Integer = 49157
  db3-initial-seq : Integer = 305419896

  db3-default-send : Text = "codex-diag-b3"

 `sendx=` REPEATS THE SEND AND EXISTS FOR ONE ARM. `short` is reachable only
 through the retransmit queue, which holds `net-rexmit-capacity` segments;
 the default payload is thirteen bytes and fills one, so before this knob no
 DIAG.CFG could drive the stage to the state the row carries and `short` was
 a word that had never been said.

 IT REPEATS THE CALL, NOT THE BUFFER, and that is the whole design. Building
 one payload above the 11,200-byte cap would mean 4096 rounds of `&` into an
 accumulator on a bump allocator with no GC, which is CostModel 3.6's
 quadratic shape and a wedge of our own making inside a stage whose entire
 purpose is to not wedge. Each repeat is a fresh send of the same small
 buffer and allocates nothing extra, while filling one queue slot apiece,
 which is the quantity the state is actually about.

 The cap is `net-rexmit-capacity`-shaped rather than a round number: enough
 repeats to overfill the queue and stop, and few enough that a cfg typo
 cannot turn the stage into a flood.

  db3-send-max-repeats : Integer = 8192

  db3-send-repeats : DiagCtx -> Integer
  db3-send-repeats (c) =
    let n = text-to-integer (db3-field c "sendx" "1")
    in if n < 1 then 1
    else if n > db3-send-max-repeats then db3-send-max-repeats
    else n

 The loop stops at the first incomplete send and carries the partial count,
 because the queue does not empty on its own inside this stage: once the drain
 has spent its budget with the queue full, every further repeat would spend it
 again for nothing.

  db3-send-times : TcpTransportState, List Integer, Integer, Integer -> [Network.Read, Network.Write] NetSendResult
  db3-send-times (ts) (payload) (left) (acc) =
   if left <= 0 then NetSendResult { ns-transport = ts, ns-sent = acc, ns-complete = True }
   else act
    r <- net-io-send-raw-checked ts payload
    if r.ns-complete == False then NetSendResult { ns-transport = r.ns-transport, ns-sent = acc + r.ns-sent, ns-complete = False }
    else db3-send-times (r.ns-transport) payload (left - 1) (acc + r.ns-sent)
   end

 THE DHCP BUDGET IS THE ARP BUDGET'S SHAPE, for the same reason: one DISCOVER
 per round, so the fuel per round IS the request rate. `dhcp-io-await` spends
 one fuel unit per empty poll, the same poll `nicsit` measures, so eight million
 is about 104 ms of listening in the bed and 263 ms on metal. Sixteen rounds
 inside a four-second deadline puts at most sixteen broadcasts on the wire in
 the bed; on metal the deadline binds first, at about fifteen. The tests reach for
 200,000 (`codex/test/dhcp-acquire.codex`), which is 2.6 ms and passes only
 because the bed answers instantly; on a segment with a real server that
 number is a null result waiting to happen.

 EACH ROUND IS A NEW TRANSACTION, not a retransmission of the last one, so
 the xid moves with the round. A server that already offered on the old xid is
 answering a conversation we are no longer having.

 NOTHING HERE RESTORES THE HEAP BETWEEN ROUNDS, and it must not start. A
 DISCOVER is a SEND, and a restore taken after a send hands the stack a
 pointer into rewound heap (DevelopersGuide, "Never `__heap-restore` around a
 send"). The receive side inside `dhcp-io-await` already restores per poll,
 which is where the frames are, so the retained cost is sixteen sockets and
 sixteen built frames and not sixteen rounds of traffic.

  db3-dhcp-word : Text = "dhcp"
  db3-dhcp-fuel : Integer = 8000000
  db3-dhcp-rounds : Integer = 16
  db3-dhcp-ms : Integer = 4000
  db3-dhcp-xid : Integer = 305419896
  db3-dhcp-host : Text = "codex-diag"

  db3-field-scan : List Text, Integer, Text, Text -> Text
  db3-field-scan (parts) (i) (key) (dflt) =
   if i >= list-length parts then dflt
   else let p = list-at parts i
   in if text-starts-with p (key & "=") then substring p (text-length key + 1) (text-length p - text-length key - 1)
   else db3-field-scan parts (i + 1) key dflt

  db3-field : DiagCtx, Text, Text -> Text
  db3-field (c) (key) (dflt) = db3-field-scan (text-split (diag-cfg-value c "b3") " ") 0 key dflt

  db3-octets : List Text, Integer, List Integer -> List Integer
  db3-octets (parts) (i) (acc) =
   if i >= list-length parts then acc
   else db3-octets parts (i + 1) (list-push acc (text-to-integer (list-at parts i)))

  db3-ip : Text -> List Integer
  db3-ip (a) = db3-octets (text-split (list-at (text-split a ":") 0) ".") 0 []

  db3-port : Text -> Integer
  db3-port (a) =
   let parts = text-split a ":"
   in if list-length parts < 2 then 0 else text-to-integer (list-at parts 1)

  db3-ip-text : List Integer -> Text
  db3-ip-text (ip) =
   if list-length ip /= 4 then "?"
   else show (list-at ip 0) & "." & show (list-at ip 1) & "." & show (list-at ip 2) & "." & show (list-at ip 3)

  db3-mac-loop : List Integer, Integer, Text -> Text
  db3-mac-loop (m) (i) (acc) =
   if i >= list-length m then acc
   else db3-mac-loop m (i + 1) (acc & (if i == 0 then "" else ":") & diag-hex2 (list-at m i))

  db3-mac-text : List Integer -> Text
  db3-mac-text (m) = if list-length m /= 6 then "none" else db3-mac-loop m 0 ""

Section: The Step Line

 A STEP LINE GOES TO BOTH CHANNELS, and until 2026-08-21 this chapter only
 sent it to one. `print-line-uni` is SERIAL. **The ASUS has no serial port**
 (`HardwareSitting.md`), the rig is a phone camera, and `diag-arm.ps1` excludes
 step lines from the bank by design because the step they name may not return.
 So on metal the instrument that says which step b3 is inside could not fire at
 all, and when b3 hung on the 2026-08-21 flight the glass said `b3 running` and
 nothing else. That is L-CHANNEL: the campaign had no output channel
 independent of the subsystem under test, and it cost the asde stage and the
 summary as well as the answer.

 `DiagAsde` had already solved this and this chapter did not follow it. What is
 copied here, invariant included: **row 0 is the step and nothing else ever
 paints there**, and a step line CLEARS THE WHOLE SLOT before taking row 0.
 DiagAsde's prose records why, and it was bought with a bed photograph: rolling
 lines through every row made the standing lines appear out of order, so a
 reader at the board could not tell which was last, which defeats the one
 property the slot exists for.

 IT PAINTS ITS SLOT, and until 2026-08-21 a non-picture stage could not. The
 grant in `Diag.codex` handed every stage that is not one of the two picture
 stages **x = dc-w + 8 and w = -16**, at every width, because the two `dc-w`
 terms cancelled. Measured off the bed with this stage at 1024x768: x=1032,
 w=-16, cols=-3. A fill of negative width paints nothing and a fitter given
 negative columns answers the empty string, so the first version of this section
 compiled clean, printed its serial line, and put NOTHING on the glass; a
 deliberately red fill was invisible too, which is what turned a theory into a
 measurement. The grant is fixed at the source and `dg-slot-refuses` now says so
 out loud rather than painting nothing, so this reads its slot like any other
 stage.

 The slot a non-picture stage gets IS ITS ROW, so this overwrites the
 `<name> running` line `dg-paint-running` put there. That is deliberate: the row
 then reads `b3 -> dhcp`, which says the stage AND the step, and the result
 overwrites it again when the stage returns.

  db3-cols : DiagCtx -> Integer
  db3-cols (c) = ((c.dc-slot-w) - 8) / (8 * (c.dc-scale))

  db3-fit : DiagCtx, Text -> Text
  db3-fit (c) (s) =
   let n = db3-cols c
   in if n < 4 then ""
   else if text-length s <= n then s
   else substring s 0 (n - 3) & "..."

  db3-at : DiagCtx, Text, Integer -> Integer
  db3-at (c) (s) (col) =
   let cleared = gop-fill-rect (c.dc-base) (c.dc-stride) (c.dc-slot-x) (c.dc-slot-y) (c.dc-slot-w) (c.dc-slot-h) diag-col-bg
   in gop-draw-text (c.dc-base) (c.dc-stride) (c.dc-font) (c.dc-slot-x) (c.dc-slot-y) (db3-fit c s) col (c.dc-scale) 0

  db3-open : DiagCtx, Text -> Integer
  db3-open (c) (s) = db3-at c s diag-col-warn

  db3-cell-notes : Integer = 92
  db3-cell-lost : Integer = 93

  db3-step : DiagCtx, Text -> [Console, Device.Port] Integer
  db3-step (c) (s) = act
   let h = __heap-save
   in let k = xdiag-get db3-cell-notes + 1
   in let counted = xdiag-put db3-cell-notes k
   in act
    n <- diag-bank-note c ("stage=b3 step=" & s & " heap=" & show h)
    p <- print-line-uni ("b3 entering " & s & " heap=" & show h & " banked=" & show n)
    if n < 0 & c.dc-bank then db3-lost c s k
    else db3-open c ("b3 -> " & s)
   end
  end

  db3-lost : DiagCtx, Text, Integer -> [Console, Device.Port] Integer
  db3-lost (c) (s) (k) = act
   let first = if xdiag-get db3-cell-lost == 0 then xdiag-put db3-cell-lost k else 0
   in act
    p <- print-line-uni ("b3 bank lost at " & s)
    db3-at c ("BANK LOST AT " & s) diag-col-bad
   end
  end

  db3-stamp-lost : DiagResult -> DiagResult
  db3-stamp-lost (r) =
   let n = xdiag-get db3-cell-lost
   in if n == 0 then r
   else let tag = "bank-lost-note=" & show n
   in diag-result (r.dr-state) diag-col-bad (db3-tag-first (r.dr-glass) tag) (db3-tag-first (r.dr-bank) tag)

  db3-tag-first : List Text, Text -> List Text
  db3-tag-first (xs) (tag) =
   when xs
    is Nil -> [tag]
    is Cons (h) (t) -> (h & " " & tag) :: t
   end

Section: The Budgets

 NetIO counts empty polls where another stack would read a clock, so a
 duration here is expressed as a number of NetIO ticks and converted through
 the interval NetDriver measured at bring-up. Starting a wait loop at a high
 try count is how a caller spends less than the full fuel cap: the loop's own
 give-up ladder and its per-tick retransmit are unchanged, only the ceiling
 is nearer.

  db3-connect-ticks : Integer = 100
  db3-reply-ticks : Integer = 100

 THE CAP IS ABSOLUTE AND DOES NOT TRUST THE TICK INTERVAL, which is the fuel cap
 this stage did not have when it hung on the 2026-08-21 flight and took asde and
 the summary with it (campaign rule 2: no stage may end the run).

 Every loop on this stage's path is bounded by a COUNTER and none of them is an
 infinite loop: the ARP and DHCP waits count rounds down, `na-await-lu` carries
 fuel, and every NetIO wait stops at `tries > net-io-max-polls`. What is not
 bounded is the SIZE of that bound, and every loop can remain formally
 terminating while the ceiling is astronomical. That is why a first reading of
 this said "no NetIO wait can hang, 3.29 s worst case" and was wrong in the
 direction that matters.

 THE ORDINARY CASE IS ALREADY THE BAD ONE, and this paragraph had it wrong until
 blu corrected it (main 18389). It said the runaway ceiling needed uninitialised
 memory, on the reasoning that `net-driver-poll-interval` reads a cell with
 `peek-32` while the calibration's fuel bounds what it writes. The read is
 indeed unbounded, but the WRITE is not small either: `net-driver-cal-loop` adds
 `cal-batch` per iteration, so a perfectly ordinary calibration can write
 200,000,000, not the 200,000 the fuel constant reads like. That is
 `net-io-max-polls` of 1e11, about 55 minutes for ONE wait at the 32.9 ns metal
 poll `nicsit` measures, from a board doing nothing unusual. No uninitialised
 memory is required to produce the flight's hang, and attributing it to
 L-FREEDOM would have sent the next reader to a board-only cause for something
 the ordinary path does. The uninitialised cell is a further worst case on top,
 not the mechanism.

 blu has clamped `net-io-max-polls` itself at main 18389, with an arm
 (`codex/test/net-poll-clamped`, three states, proven to fail without the fix),
 so the ceiling is bounded for every caller now and not only for this stage.

 So the ceiling is computed from an ABSOLUTE number of polls rather than from
 the interval. 90,000,000 polls is about 3.0 s at that measured metal poll. The
 loop's give-up ladder and its per-tick retransmit are unchanged, as before;
 only the ceiling is now ours.

 THE CLAMP IS NOT MERELY DEFENSIVE: IT ENGAGES IN THE BED TODAY, and the first
 version of this paragraph said the opposite. It claimed the bed always falls to
 the 100000 fallback so the clamp could never fire, and one run refuted it:
 `poll-interval=1758000 spend=90000000 cap=90000000 clamped=y`. The bed
 CALIBRATES to about 1.76 million, so `want` is 175,800,000 and the cap halves
 it. Uncapped, `net-io-max-polls` here is 879,000,000 and each of b3's two
 budgeted waits would spend 175.8 million polls: 2.3 s at the bed's 13.0 ns and
 5.8 s on metal, per wait. The measurement was one row away the whole time and
 the assumption was written first, which is the habit this file keeps paying
 for.

 THE CEILING REPORTS ITSELF. The head row paints the interval it read, the spend
 it settled on, the cap, and whether the clamp engaged, so the ceiling a run
 actually used is a reading rather than something inferred from the fact that
 the run finished. It was added to make an unfalsifiable defence measurable and
 it immediately refuted the paragraph above it, which is the best argument for
 painting a number instead of reasoning about it.

 WHAT THIS CAP DOES NOT REACH. `db3-start-at` is passed to the CONNECT and
 RECEIVE waits and to nothing else. The send path takes no start value:
 `net-io-send-chunk` calls `net-io-send-drain ts 0 0`, and blu's second
 correction is that the drain runs ONCE PER CHUNK, so a stalled peer pays the
 whole ceiling per 1400 bytes rather than once for the send. Capping that from a
 caller is not possible and it is not this stage's to fix; blu's clamp on
 `net-io-max-polls` is what bounds it, for every caller.

  db3-max-spend : Integer = 90000000

  db3-spend : Integer -> Integer
  db3-spend (ticks) =
   let want = net-io-tick-interval * ticks
   in if want > db3-max-spend then db3-max-spend else want

  db3-start-at : Integer -> Integer
  db3-start-at (ticks) =
   let n = net-io-max-polls - db3-spend ticks
   in if n < 0 then 0 else n

  db3-connect : TcpTransportState -> [Network.Read, Network.Write] TcpTransportState
  db3-connect (ts) =
   let ts1 = transport-connect ts
   in act
    ts2 <- flush-transport-outbox ts1
    net-io-wait-established-poll ts2 (db3-start-at db3-connect-ticks) 0
   end

Section: The Next Hop

 The session sends every frame to the ARP entry for its GATEWAY address
 (`resolve-dst-mac`, NetworkStack) and falls back to broadcast when it has
 none, so a peer on the same segment is reached by naming it as the next hop.
 `gw=` overrides it; absent, the next hop is the peer itself, which is right
 for a desk box and wrong for anything off the segment.

 THE REQUEST IS RE-SENT EVERY ROUND. One ARP request lost on a real switch
 would otherwise read as `no-arp`, which is a state that says the segment is
 broken rather than that one frame went missing.

  db3-arp-chunk : List Integer, Integer -> [Network.Read] List Integer
  db3-arp-chunk (target) (n) =
   if n <= 0 then []
   else let hp = __heap-save
   in act
    frame <- net-driver-recv-frame
    if list-length frame == 0 then let r = __heap-restore hp in db3-arp-chunk target (n - 1)
    else if eth-ethertype frame /= eth-type-arp then let r2 = __heap-restore hp in db3-arp-chunk target (n - 1)
    else let pkt = arp-parse (eth-payload frame)
    in if (pkt.op == arp-op-reply) & ip-equal (pkt.sender-ip) target then pkt.sender-mac
    else let r3 = __heap-restore hp in db3-arp-chunk target (n - 1)
   end

  db3-arp-wait : List Integer, List Integer, List Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> [Network.Read, Network.Write] List Integer
  db3-arp-wait (mac) (src-ip) (target) (start) (want) (rounds) =
   if rounds <= 0 then []
   else let hp = __heap-save
   in act
    sent <- net-driver-send-frame (arp-build-request mac src-ip target)
    m <- db3-arp-chunk target db3-chunk
    if list-length m == 6 then m
    else let r = __heap-restore hp
    in if (hpet-ticks - start) >= want then []
    else db3-arp-wait mac src-ip target start want (rounds - 1)
   end

Section: The Reply

  db3-bytes-text : List Integer, Integer, Text -> Text
  db3-bytes-text (b) (i) (acc) =
   if i >= list-length b then acc
   else if i >= 48 then acc
   else let v = list-at b i
   in db3-bytes-text b (i + 1) (acc & (if v < 32 then "." else if v > 126 then "." else char-to-text (code-to-char (from-unicode v))))

  db3-bytes-hex : List Integer, Integer, Text -> Text
  db3-bytes-hex (b) (i) (acc) =
   if i >= list-length b then acc
   else if i >= 16 then acc
   else db3-bytes-hex b (i + 1) (acc & diag-hex2 (list-at b i))

Section: The Lease

  db3-lease-text : DhcpLease -> Text
  db3-lease-text (l) =
   "ip=" & db3-ip-text (l.client-ip) & " gw=" & db3-ip-text (l.gateway) & " dns=" & db3-ip-text (l.dns-server) & " lease=" & show (l.lease-time)

  db3-dhcp-wait : List Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> [Network.Read, Network.Write] DhcpLease
  db3-dhcp-wait (mac) (start) (want) (rounds) =
   if rounds <= 0 then dhcp-lease-empty
   else act
    lease <- dhcp-io-acquire mac (db3-dhcp-xid + rounds) db3-dhcp-fuel
    if dhcp-is-ack lease then lease
    else if (hpet-ticks - start) >= want then dhcp-lease-empty
    else db3-dhcp-wait mac start want (rounds - 1)
   end

Section: The Stage

 THE NEXT HOP IS PASSED IN AND NOT RECOMPUTED HERE. It decides two different
 things -- who we ARP for, and the address the session resolves every frame's
 destination MAC against -- and a stage that derived it twice could ARP for
 one address and address its frames to another after any edit to the default,
 answering ok while proving nothing about the peer that was named.

  db3-talk : DiagCtx, Integer, List Integer, List Integer, List Integer, List Integer, Integer, Integer, Text -> [Device.Port, Device.Mmio, Console, Network.Read, Network.Write] DiagResult
  db3-talk (c) (link) (src-ip) (peer-ip) (hop-ip) (hop-mac) (peer-port) (hz) (head) = act
   say <- db3-step c "connect"
   let sess = net-session-new net-driver-mac src-ip hop-ip db3-src-port peer-port peer-ip db3-initial-seq
   in let seeded = set-arp sess (arp-cache-add (sess.arp) hop-ip hop-mac)
   in act
    ts1 <- db3-connect (transport-new seeded)
    when (ts1.session).conn.state
     is TcpEstablished -> act
       say2 <- db3-step c "exchange"
       let send-text = db3-field c "send" db3-default-send
       in let payload = text-to-bytes-chunk send-text 0 (text-length send-text) []
       in act
        sr <- db3-send-times ts1 payload (db3-send-repeats c) 0
        r <- net-io-recv-raw (sr.ns-transport) (db3-start-at db3-reply-ticks)
        ts3 <- net-io-close (r.transport)
        let n = list-length (r.data)
        in let want = db3-field c "expect" ""
        in let seen = db3-bytes-text (r.data) 0 ""
        in let state = if sr.ns-complete == False then "short"
              else if r.has-data == False then "no-reply"
              else if text-length want == 0 then "ok"
              else if text-contains seen want then "ok"
              else "mismatch"
        in let colour = if state == "ok" then diag-col-good else if state == "no-reply" then diag-col-warn else diag-col-bad
        in let answer = "pe=" & db3-ip-text peer-ip & ":" & show peer-port & " hop=" & db3-mac-text hop-mac & " sent=" & show (sr.ns-sent) & "/" & show (list-length payload * db3-send-repeats c) & " rx=" & show n & " lk=" & show link
        in let body = "reply bytes=" & show n & " hex=" & db3-bytes-hex (r.data) 0 "" & " text=" & seen
        in let cfg = "cfg src=" & db3-ip-text src-ip & " hop-ip=" & db3-ip-text hop-ip & " expect=" & (if text-length want == 0 then "(none)" else want) & " hpet=" & show hz
        in diag-result state colour [answer, body] [answer, head, cfg, body]
       end
     end
     is otherwise -> act
       ts2 <- net-io-close ts1
       let answer = "pe=" & db3-ip-text peer-ip & ":" & show peer-port & " hop=" & db3-mac-text hop-mac & " sent=0 rx=0 lk=" & show link
       in diag-result "refused" diag-col-bad [answer, "handshake never reached established"] [answer, head, "handshake never reached established"]
     end
   end
  end

  db3-after-address : DiagCtx, Integer, List Integer, List Integer, Integer, Integer, Text -> [Device.Port, Device.Mmio, Console, Network.Read, Network.Write] DiagResult
  db3-after-address (c) (link) (src-ip) (peer-ip) (peer-port) (hz) (head) = act
   say2 <- db3-step c "arp"
   let hop-ip = db3-ip (db3-field c "gw" (db3-ip-text peer-ip))
   in let s0 = hpet-ticks
   in act
    hop-mac <- db3-arp-wait net-driver-mac src-ip hop-ip s0 ((hz * 2000) / 1000) db3-arp-rounds
    if list-length hop-mac /= 6
     then diag-result "no-arp" diag-col-bad ["hop=" & db3-ip-text hop-ip & " unanswered lk=" & show link] [head, "no ARP reply for " & db3-ip-text hop-ip & " in 2000 ms"]
     else db3-talk c link src-ip peer-ip hop-ip hop-mac peer-port hz head
   end
  end

  db3-with-dhcp : DiagCtx, Integer, List Integer, Integer, Integer, Text -> [Device.Port, Device.Mmio, Console, Network.Read, Network.Write] DiagResult
  db3-with-dhcp (c) (link) (peer-ip) (peer-port) (hz) (head) = act
   say3 <- db3-step c "dhcp"
   let d0 = hpet-ticks
   in act
    lease <- db3-dhcp-wait net-driver-mac d0 ((hz * db3-dhcp-ms) / 1000) db3-dhcp-rounds
    if dhcp-is-ack lease == False
     then diag-result "no-lease" diag-col-bad ["ip=dhcp and no server answered in " & show db3-dhcp-ms & " ms lk=" & show link] [head, "ip=dhcp was asked for and no DHCP server answered in " & show db3-dhcp-ms & " ms across " & show db3-dhcp-rounds & " DISCOVERs; the address was asked for FROM the segment, so this is the segment's answer and not a DIAG.CFG mistake"]
     else db3-after-address c link (lease.client-ip) peer-ip peer-port hz (head & " addr=dhcp " & db3-lease-text lease)
   end
  end

  db3-clock-reads : Integer = 100000

  db3-clock-spin : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  db3-clock-spin (t0) (i) (n) (moved) =
   if i >= n then moved
   else let v = hpet-ticks
   in db3-clock-spin t0 (i + 1) n (if v /= t0 then moved + 1 else moved)

  db3-clock-line : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Text
  db3-clock-line (hz) (t0) (t1) (moved) =
   "clk=" & (if t1 > t0 then "y" else "n") & " dt=" & show (t1 - t0) & " moved=" & show moved & "/" & show db3-clock-reads & " hpet=" & show hz

 The seven operations of `e1000-reset`, in its order, each painted and banked
 before it runs: `NicInitProbe` runs the same seven at stage 12 in
 milliseconds and sitting 10 hung inside them here at stage 14, same code,
 different part state, and the only instrument that can say WHICH operation
 is one that banks between them. The value returned is what `e1000-reset`
 returns, `settled`, so bring-up's absent decision is the driver's own.

  db3-reset : DiagCtx, Integer -> [Console, Device.Port] Integer
  db3-reset (c) (mmio) = act
   r1 <- db3-step c "reset-imc"
   let m1 = e1000-write mmio e1000-reg-imc #FFFFFFFF
   in act
    r2 <- db3-step c "reset-ctrl-read"
    let ctrl = e1000-read mmio e1000-reg-ctrl
    in act
     r3 <- db3-step c ("reset-rst-write ctrl=" & show ctrl)
     let w = e1000-write mmio e1000-reg-ctrl (bit-or ctrl e1000-ctrl-rst)
     in act
      r4 <- db3-step c "reset-await-reset"
      let settled = e1000-await-reset mmio 0
      in act
       r5 <- db3-step c ("reset-settle-mdio settled=" & show settled)
       let waited = e1000-settle-mdio mmio
       in act
        r6 <- db3-step c "reset-imc-again"
        let m2 = e1000-write mmio e1000-reg-imc #FFFFFFFF
        in act
         r7 <- db3-step c "reset-icr"
         let cleared = e1000-read mmio e1000-reg-icr
         in settled
        end
       end
      end
     end
    end
   end
  end

 `e1000-init-after-reset` in its order, each part painted and banked before
 it runs, because sitting 11's medium died between the `rings-link` note and
 the `k1` note and this function is everything between them. Of its parts,
 the SWFLAG acquire (2,000 read-modify-writes of EXTCNF_CTRL with MNG held)
 and the CTRL|SLU write in `e1000-link-up` are the two `nicinit`'s bring-up
 never did; the ring setup is what it did under a receiver that was never
 quiesced. The record is rebuilt verbatim from the driver's constructor, so a
 field added there refuses here at compile time rather than drifting.

  db3-init-after-reset : DiagCtx, Integer -> [Console, Device.Port] E1000Device
  db3-init-after-reset (c) (mmio) = act
   i1 <- db3-step c "rings-quiesce"
   let quiet = e1000-quiesce mmio
   in let rx-ring = e1000-alloc-aligned (e1000-rx-count * e1000-desc-size) 16
   in let tx-ring = e1000-alloc-aligned (e1000-tx-count * e1000-desc-size) 16
   in let rx-bufs = e1000-alloc-aligned (e1000-rx-count * e1000-buf-size) 16
   in let tx-bufs = e1000-alloc-aligned (e1000-tx-count * e1000-buf-size) 16
   in let ctrl-blk = e1000-alloc-aligned e1000-cb-size 8
   in let cb-clear = e1000-zero-words ctrl-blk (e1000-cb-size / 4) 0
   in let mta = e1000-clear-mta mmio 0
   in let mac-ok = e1000-mac-present mmio
   in let mac = if mac-ok then e1000-read-mac mmio else []
   in let dev = E1000Device { e-mmio = mmio, e-rx-ring = rx-ring,
                              e-tx-ring = tx-ring, e-rx-bufs = rx-bufs,
                              e-tx-bufs = tx-bufs, e-ctrl-blk = ctrl-blk,
                              e-present = True, e-mac = mac,
                              e-mac-valid = mac-ok, e-pch = 0, e-ulp = 0 }
   in act
    i2 <- db3-step c "setup-rx"
    let rx = e1000-setup-rx dev
    in act
     i3 <- db3-step c "setup-tx"
     let tx = e1000-setup-tx dev
     in act
      i4 <- db3-step c "swflag"
      let sem = if e1000-pch-swflag-required then e1000-swflag-acquire mmio e1000-swflag-fuel else 1
      in act
       i5 <- db3-step c ("link-up sem=" & show sem)
       let link = e1000-link-up mmio
       in act
        i6 <- db3-step c ("swflag-release link=" & show link)
        let rel = if e1000-pch-swflag-required then e1000-swflag-release mmio else 0
        in dev
       end
      end
     end
    end
   end
  end

  db3-init-or-absent : DiagCtx, Integer, Integer -> [Console, Device.Port] E1000Device
  db3-init-or-absent (c) (settled) (mmio) = act
   if settled == 0 then e1000-absent else db3-init-after-reset c mmio
  end

  db3-bring-up : DiagCtx, PciDevice, Integer -> [Console, Device.Port, Network.Read] (Integer, Integer)
  db3-bring-up (c) (d) (mmio) = act
   settled <- db3-reset c mmio
   dev <- db3-init-or-absent c settled mmio
   s3 <- db3-step c "k1"
   let pch = e1000-pch-prepare mmio (d.pci-device-id) e1000-pch-k1-required
   in let card = net-driver-bind-e1000 dev
   in act
    k <- diag-bank-note c ("stage=b3 k1=" & show pch)
    s4 <- db3-step c "calibrate"
    p <- net-driver-calibrate
    let w = poke-32 net-driver-poll-cell 0 p
    in (card, pch)
   end
  end

  db3-run-part : DiagCtx, PciDevice, Integer, List Integer, Integer -> [Device.Port, Device.Mmio, Console, Network.Read, Network.Write] DiagResult
  db3-run-part (c) (d) (mmio) (peer-ip) (peer-port) = act
   let started = hpet-start
   in let hz = hpet-ticks-per-second
   in if hz <= 0 then diag-result "no-hpet" diag-col-bad ["no HPET, so no wait on this path is budgeted"] ["no HPET, so no wait on this path is budgeted"]
   else act
    sayc <- db3-step c "clock"
    let t0 = hpet-ticks
    in let moved = db3-clock-spin t0 0 db3-clock-reads 0
    in let t1 = hpet-ticks
    in let clk = db3-clock-line hz t0 t1 moved
    in act
     noted <- diag-bank-note c ("stage=b3 " & clk)
     if t1 <= t0 then diag-result "clock-stuck" diag-col-bad [clk] [clk, "the HPET reports a rate and its counter did not advance across " & show db3-clock-reads & " reads, so every budget in the driver would wait forever; nothing was dialled"]
     else act
      r <- db3-bring-up c d mmio
      when r
       is (card, pch) -> act
        let ip = db3-field c "ip" ""
        in let head = "card=" & net-driver-card-name card & " mac=" & db3-mac-text net-driver-mac & " poll-interval=" & show net-io-tick-interval & " spend=" & show (db3-spend db3-connect-ticks) & " cap=" & show db3-max-spend & " clamped=" & (if db3-spend db3-connect-ticks < net-io-tick-interval * db3-connect-ticks then "y" else "n") & " " & clk & " steps=reset(7),init-after-reset(6),k1,calibrate k1=" & show pch
        in if card /= net-card-e1000 then diag-result "no-card" diag-col-bad [head, "the driver would not bind the part"] [head, "the driver would not bind the part"]
        else act
         say1 <- db3-step c "link wait"
         let link = na-link-wait mmio
         in if link == 0 then diag-result "no-link" diag-col-bad ["lk=0 " & head] [head, "link never came up inside the budget"]
         else if ip == db3-dhcp-word then db3-with-dhcp c link peer-ip peer-port hz head
         else db3-after-address c link (db3-ip ip) peer-ip peer-port hz (head & " addr=static")
        end
       end
     end
    end
   end
  end

  db3-run : DiagCtx -> [Device.Port, Device.Mmio, Console, Network.Read, Network.Write] DiagResult
  db3-run (c) = act
   let z1 = xdiag-put db3-cell-notes 0
   in let z2 = xdiag-put db3-cell-lost 0
   in act
    r <- db3-run-inner c
    db3-stamp-lost r
   end
  end

  db3-run-inner : DiagCtx -> [Device.Port, Device.Mmio, Console, Network.Read, Network.Write] DiagResult
  db3-run-inner (c) = act
   let peer = db3-field c "peer" ""
   in let ip = db3-field c "ip" ""
   in let addr-bad = if ip == db3-dhcp-word then False else list-length (db3-ip ip) /= 4
   in if text-length peer == 0 then diag-result "no-peer" diag-col-dim ["DIAG.CFG names no peer, so nothing was dialled"] ["DIAG.CFG names no peer, so nothing was dialled"]
   else if text-length ip == 0 then diag-result "no-address" diag-col-warn ["DIAG.CFG names a peer and no ip=, so there is no address to dial FROM"] ["DIAG.CFG names a peer and no ip=, so there is no address to dial FROM; this stage has no default because a default here would be a guess about the network. Type an address, or type ip=dhcp to ask the segment for one"]
   else if addr-bad then diag-result "no-address" diag-col-bad ["ip=" & ip & " does not parse to four octets"] ["ip=" & ip & " does not parse to four octets"]
   else when e1000-find (c.dc-pci)
    is None -> diag-result "no-part" diag-col-dim ["no eligible Intel part on the bus"] ["no eligible Intel part on the bus"]
    is Just (d) -> if na-eligible d == False then diag-result "rejected" diag-col-warn [na-reject-line d] [na-reject-line d]
     else if e1000-bar-verdict (na-bar-of d) /= e1000-bar-ok then diag-result "bar-bad" diag-col-bad ["verdict=" & na-verdict-text (e1000-bar-verdict (na-bar-of d)) & ", the part was not touched"] ["verdict=" & na-verdict-text (e1000-bar-verdict (na-bar-of d)) & ", the part was not touched"]
     else db3-run-part c d (na-mmio-of d) (db3-ip peer) (db3-port peer)
  end

